WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.151 --> 00:00:04.835
<v Maya>For decades, I mean, we've had this collective obsession with the Turing test, you know?</v>

2
00:00:04.915 --> 00:00:08.359
<v Daniel>Oh, right, yeah. The classic man versus machine typing game.</v>

3
00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:19.769
<v Maya>Exactly. The idea was always so simple. You sit at a computer, you type messages to some hidden entity, and, well, if you can't tell whether you're talking to a machine or a human, the machine wins.</v>

4
00:00:20.029 --> 00:00:28.187
<v Daniel>Yeah, and we always just assumed that for an AI to pass that test... It would have to actually become smarter, right? Like deeply complex.</v>

5
00:00:28.207 --> 00:00:29.970
<v Maya>Right. We thought it would have to actually understand</v>

6
00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.556
<v Daniel>us. That was the dream or, you know, maybe the nightmare, depending on who you ask.</v>

7
00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:34.337
<v Maya>Yes, seriously.</v>

8
00:00:34.817 --> 00:00:40.699
<v Daniel>The whole assumption was that mimicking humanity required this deep underlying mastery of the human experience.</v>

9
00:00:40.759 --> 00:00:46.680
<v Maya>But what if it doesn't? Like, what if fooling us doesn't require genuine intelligence or understanding at all?</v>

10
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.681
<v Daniel>Which is a terrifying thought, honestly.</v>

11
00:00:48.721 --> 00:00:57.464
<v Maya>It really is. What if appearing human is just this stylistic parlor trick? You know, just a superficial dial that a machine can simply turn up on command.</v>

12
00:00:57.684 --> 00:00:58.924
<v Daniel>And that brings us to today.</v>

13
00:00:59.511 --> 00:01:14.176
<v Maya>Yes, welcome to this deep dive. Today, we're giving you a shortcut to understanding a massive shift in how we interact with artificial intelligence. We are unpacking a brand new, frankly fascinating paper published just this week, September</v>

14
00:01:14.456 --> 00:01:16.156
<v Maya>3rd, 2026.</v>

15
00:01:16.516 --> 00:01:18.777
<v Daniel>Hot off the presses in nature communications.</v>

16
00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:28.260
<v Maya>Exactly. It's by Bennett Kleinberg and colleagues, and it's titled Perceived Humanness and Empathy are Dissociated in Relationship Advice Generated by a Large Language Model.</v>

17
00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:39.918
<v Daniel>And to give you, the listener, the full picture of why this research is sending literal shockwaves through the field right now, we're also weaving in context from five foundational papers from the last few years.</v>

18
00:01:40.178 --> 00:01:46.582
<v Maya>We really need to look back at how we got here to truly grasp what Kleinberg's team just uncovered about the mechanics of human deception.</v>

19
00:01:47.261 --> 00:01:50.403
<v Daniel>Because the central mystery here is incredibly counterintuitive.</v>

20
00:01:50.603 --> 00:02:01.931
<v Maya>It really is. Basically, if you ask an AI to sound more human, it can do it instantly. It adapts. But if you take a living, breathing human being and give them that exact same instruction, sound more human, they completely fail.</v>

21
00:02:02.311 --> 00:02:04.152
<v Daniel>It just sounds absurd on its face.</v>

22
00:02:04.432 --> 00:02:16.080
<v Maya>Right. So we're going to break down exactly how the AI pulls off this illusion, why we keep falling for it, and the very real consequences this has for our own personal relationships.</v>

23
00:02:16.387 --> 00:02:20.931
<v Daniel>But to really appreciate this new breakthrough, I think we have to start by correcting the record.</v>

24
00:02:21.291 --> 00:02:26.215
<v Maya>Yeah, we have to look at the flawed narrative this 2026 paper is actively dismantling.</v>

25
00:02:26.615 --> 00:02:35.842
<v Daniel>So that older narrative really took hold a few years ago. There was this highly influential study back in 2023 by J.K. Hancock and Naaman.</v>

26
00:02:36.097 --> 00:02:38.858
<v Maya>I remember that one. It caused a huge panic in the media.</v>

27
00:02:39.139 --> 00:02:46.082
<v Daniel>Oh, massive panic. It famously claimed that AI generated text could actually see more human than text written by actual people.</v>

28
00:02:46.262 --> 00:02:51.425
<v Maya>Right. It suggested our mental shortcuts for detecting AI were totally broken, that the machines had already beaten us.</v>

29
00:02:51.525 --> 00:03:02.253
<v Daniel>Exactly. But Kleinberg's new research looks at those older studies and basically throws a yellow flag on the play. They argue the conditions of those tests were entirely unfair to the human participants.</v>

30
00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:03.393
<v Maya>Unfair how? Exactly.</v>

31
00:03:03.614 --> 00:03:13.021
<v Daniel>Well, if you look at what people were actually asked to write in those older studies, it was things like online dating profiles or, you know, professional bios for LinkedIn.</v>

32
00:03:13.301 --> 00:03:16.323
<v Maya>Oh, wow. I mean, those are inherently low empathy tasks.</v>

33
00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:21.267
<v Daniel>Right. Writing a professional bio is literally just self-promotion. You don't have to connect with another mind.</v>

34
00:03:21.565 --> 00:03:28.353
<v Maya>And furthermore, the human writers in those older studies, they had absolutely no idea they were in a Turing-style competition, right?</v>

35
00:03:28.373 --> 00:03:39.565
<v Daniel>Not at all. They were just going about their business, writing normal bios. Meanwhile, the AI was actively being prompted and optimized to generate highly relatable human-sounding text.</v>

36
00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:47.969
<v Maya>That's wild. It's like judging a foot race where the human thinks they're just out for a casual Sunday jog, but the AI is wearing spikes and waiting for the starting pistol.</v>

37
00:03:48.169 --> 00:03:49.870
<v Daniel>Of course the human lost that race.</v>

38
00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:56.552
<v Maya>Yeah. So that imbalance is what makes Kleinberg's new approach so vital. They decided to level the playing field.</v>

39
00:03:56.733 --> 00:04:04.256
<v Daniel>First off, they chose a task that demands real perspective taking and emotional intelligence. They chose giving relationship advice.</v>

40
00:04:04.775 --> 00:04:11.082
<v Maya>Which is brilliant because it's not just about listing accomplishments. It is about reading a situation and responding to vulnerability.</v>

41
00:04:11.303 --> 00:04:21.755
<v Daniel>Precisely. And second, they made it a fair fight. They explicitly instructed both the human writers and the GPT-4 model to, quote, sound as human as possible.</v>

42
00:04:22.197 --> 00:04:28.622
<v Maya>Okay, so the playing field is level. The human knows they're in a competition. The task requires genuine empathy. Do we win?</v>

43
00:04:28.882 --> 00:04:37.048
<v Daniel>We actually do. Across the first three studies in this paper, when you just look at the baseline, human writers still read as more human overall.</v>

44
00:04:37.469 --> 00:04:38.449
<v Maya>Okay, that's a relief.</v>

45
00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:48.257
<v Daniel>Yeah. The statistical advantage for humans was robust. They measured a Cohen's d of about 0.82 to 1.02 across the studies. Readers could generally tell the difference.</v>

46
00:04:48.442 --> 00:04:51.684
<v Maya>But I'm guessing that victory is pretty fragile based on where this is going.</v>

47
00:04:51.764 --> 00:05:00.888
<v Daniel>Very fragile because of what happens next. Remember that explicit instruction to sound human? When told to do that, only the language model actually complied.</v>

48
00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:02.069
<v Maya>Wait, what?</v>

49
00:05:02.129 --> 00:05:08.672
<v Daniel>Yeah. GPT-4's perceived humanness scores shot up by a massive 36 to 41 percent.</v>

50
00:05:08.752 --> 00:05:17.377
<v Maya>36 to 41 percent. That is a staggering jump from just a few words in a prompt. The AI essentially shapeshifts on command.</v>

51
00:05:17.732 --> 00:05:18.574
<v Daniel>It totally does.</v>

52
00:05:18.694 --> 00:05:26.767
<v Maya>But what about the human writers? I mean, if I'm told to sound human, I assume I turn up the charm, use some slang, maybe be a bit more conversational.</v>

53
00:05:27.345 --> 00:05:32.148
<v Daniel>You would think so, but the data says otherwise. The human riders moved by essentially nothing.</v>

54
00:05:32.488 --> 00:05:34.150
<v Maya>Nothing. Literally zero.</v>

55
00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:48.939
<v Daniel>Statistically, their changes hovered between minus 1.4 and plus 2.6%. It was just noise. Wow. The human attempt to sound human had zero effect on the reader. And readers' ability to tell the difference just collapses.</v>

56
00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.662
<v Daniel>The diagnostic area under the curve falls drastically from around 0.83 down to 0.62.</v>

57
00:05:56.204 --> 00:06:00.510
<v Maya>Let me push back on that, though. Could this just be like a psychological freeze response?</v>

58
00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:01.051
<v Daniel>How do you mean?</v>

59
00:06:01.411 --> 00:06:10.925
<v Maya>Well, if someone tells me an actual human being to sound human, I might overthink it. I'd sit there staring at a blank screen wondering, you know, how do I normally type? It's a really confusing prompt for a person.</v>

60
00:06:11.169 --> 00:06:17.757
<v Daniel>That is a great point, and the researchers anticipated that exact critique. That's why Study 3 is so elegantly designed.</v>

61
00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:18.978
<v Maya>Oh, what did they do?</v>

62
00:06:19.299 --> 00:06:29.231
<v Daniel>They introduced an evasion condition. Instead of using that confusing prompt to sound human, they simply told the human writers, avoid sounding like an AI.</v>

63
00:06:29.957 --> 00:06:38.119
<v Maya>Ah, okay. That is a highly concrete, actionable instruction. Just don't sound robotic. I could work with that. I'd probably throw in some conversational filler or something.</v>

64
00:06:38.339 --> 00:06:40.399
<v Daniel>Right, but the result was identical.</v>

65
00:06:40.599 --> 00:06:41.460
<v Maya>Seriously?</v>

66
00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:52.042
<v Daniel>Yeah, even with the evasion instruction, human writers still didn't change their perceived humanness scores at all. And the model, meanwhile, successfully manipulated its scores once again.</v>

67
00:06:52.282 --> 00:06:59.091
<v Maya>So humans simply do not have a dial they can turn to adjust how human they seem in text. We just write the way we write.</v>

68
00:06:59.312 --> 00:07:08.062
<v Daniel>Exactly. And to your earlier point about the task itself giving relationship advice, study two proves that empathy is the only real high ground we have left.</v>

69
00:07:08.558 --> 00:07:09.418
<v Maya>How did they prove that?</v>

70
00:07:09.578 --> 00:07:18.262
<v Daniel>They tested what happens when you remove the need for empathy. They asked participants to merely describe a relationship rather than giving advice about one. Just</v>

71
00:07:18.562 --> 00:07:19.403
<v Maya>clinical description.</v>

72
00:07:19.503 --> 00:07:27.126
<v Daniel>Right. And when it shifted to that descriptive task, the human advantage was literally sliced in half. It went from a Coman's D of 1.01 down to 0.46.</v>

73
00:07:29.407 --> 00:07:34.411
<v Maya>Oh, wow. That's a huge drop. So empathy really is the vehicle that carries our humanity in text.</v>

74
00:07:34.591 --> 00:07:39.755
<v Daniel>It is. Which brings us to what I think is the most fascinating paradox in this entire deep dive.</v>

75
00:07:39.875 --> 00:07:40.635
<v Maya>Okay. Let's hear it.</v>

76
00:07:41.156 --> 00:07:50.943
<v Daniel>If we just establish that empathy is our core vehicle for humanness, then logically, if you instruct an AI to be incredibly empathic, it should become the ultimate fake human.</v>

77
00:07:51.376 --> 00:07:53.817
<v Maya>Right. It should fool absolutely everyone.</v>

78
00:07:53.837 --> 00:08:05.059
<v Daniel>Yeah, is the logical assumption. And it sets up the massive twist in study four. The researchers took GBT-40 and explicitly instructed it to use three distinct types of empathy in its advice.</v>

79
00:08:05.239 --> 00:08:06.359
<v Maya>Okay. What are the three types?</v>

80
00:08:06.659 --> 00:08:17.682
<v Daniel>First, cognitive empathy, which is understanding the other person's feelings. Second, emotional empathy, actually sharing in those feelings. And third, motivational empathy, showing a drive to actually help.</v>

81
00:08:18.086 --> 00:08:20.832
<v Maya>So basically, the holy trinity of being a supportive friend.</v>

82
00:08:21.032 --> 00:08:35.257
<v Daniel>Exactly. And the model followed those instructions beautifully. When human readers evaluated these AI texts, they scored in this genuinely more empathic across all three dimensions. The AI successfully delivered profound,</v>

83
00:08:35.957 --> 00:08:37.138
<v Daniel>multilayered empathy.</v>

84
00:08:37.238 --> 00:08:42.323
<v Maya>So the people reading it really felt supported. They felt the empathy radiating off the screen.</v>

85
00:08:42.363 --> 00:08:48.468
<v Daniel>They did. Yet paradoxically, those highly empathic texts were rated by readers as less human.</v>

86
00:08:48.548 --> 00:08:51.729
<v Maya>Wait, hold on. They rated it as deeply empathetic, but less human.</v>

87
00:08:51.889 --> 00:08:54.910
<v Daniel>Yes. There was a notable drop in perceived humanness.</v>

88
00:08:54.950 --> 00:09:02.091
<v Maya>That completely breaks my mental model of how we communicate. The more empathetic the text became, the more robotic it felt to the reader.</v>

89
00:09:02.251 --> 00:09:04.972
<v Daniel>That is exactly what happened. And this is a crucial takeaway for you listening.</v>

90
00:09:05.052 --> 00:09:05.292
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

91
00:09:05.312 --> 00:09:11.714
<v Daniel>The language model can produce empathy on demand and it can produce humanness on demand. But these are two entirely separate dials on the machine.</v>

92
00:09:12.014 --> 00:09:12.414
<v Maya>Oh, I see.</v>

93
00:09:12.454 --> 00:09:17.495
<v Daniel>Turning up the empathy dial does not automatically turn up the humanness dial. In fact, it actively turns it down.</v>

94
00:09:17.905 --> 00:09:28.292
<v Maya>But why? I mean, if it's scoring off the charts on empathy, but people think it's a machine, what is it actually generating? Because it's clearly not utilizing a real human emotional experience.</v>

95
00:09:28.352 --> 00:09:32.615
<v Daniel>Well, the authors introduce a brilliant term for this. Stochastic empathy.</v>

96
00:09:32.695 --> 00:09:34.937
<v Maya>Stochastic empathy. I like that.</v>

97
00:09:35.191 --> 00:09:47.142
<v Daniel>To unpack that, we really have to reference a famous 2021 paper by Emily Bender, Timnit Gebru, and their colleagues. They coined the term stochastic parrots to describe these large language models.</v>

98
00:09:47.242 --> 00:09:49.384
<v Maya>Right, the stochastic parrots paper.</v>

99
00:09:49.584 --> 00:09:59.293
<v Daniel>Yeah, the argument there is that these models are just mindlessly stitching words together based on statistical probabilities. They have no grounding in the real world, no actual understanding of meaning.</v>

100
00:09:59.393 --> 00:10:10.237
<v Maya>It's like a parrot mimicking the sound of a barking dog. The parrot doesn't know what a dog is. It doesn't understand territory or danger. It just knows that making that specific noise gets a reaction.</v>

101
00:10:10.637 --> 00:10:25.543
<v Daniel>Precisely. So stochastic empathy means the AI is generating the perfect, statistically probable, surface-level language of empathy. It knows all the right phrases to validate your feelings, but it has absolutely no access</v>

102
00:10:25.663 --> 00:10:27.084
<v Daniel>to what empathy is actually for.</v>

103
00:10:27.284 --> 00:10:29.847
<v Maya>Because it has never felt pain or heartbreak or joy.</v>

104
00:10:30.147 --> 00:10:34.031
<v Daniel>Exactly. It is a completely hollow linguistic simulation of care.</v>

105
00:10:34.191 --> 00:10:45.022
<v Maya>Let me play devil's advocate for a second, though. Is calling it stochastic empathy a fair critique? Like, if it actually makes people feel better, does it really matter that it's just statistical mimicry?</v>

106
00:10:45.222 --> 00:10:46.543
<v Daniel>That's a huge debate right now.</v>

107
00:10:46.663 --> 00:10:58.792
<v Maya>Because if you think about that 2024 paper by Yin and their team, they found that AI responses absolutely make people feel heard and validated, sometimes even more than human responses, because the AI is just endlessly patient.</v>

108
00:10:58.912 --> 00:11:06.598
<v Daniel>That's a valid point. But Yin's paper also revealed a devastating caveat. The AI makes people feel wonderful right up until you apply an AI label to the text.</v>

109
00:11:06.698 --> 00:11:07.319
<v Maya>Oh, right.</v>

110
00:11:07.419 --> 00:11:12.703
<v Daniel>Yeah. The moment people realize a machine generated the advice, that positive effect is completely destroyed.</v>

111
00:11:13.012 --> 00:11:24.903
<v Maya>Which perfectly aligns with that 2025 research by Osborne and Bailey. They looked at how people subjectively evaluate advice in the dating domain, which is the exact same space as this new Kleinberg paper.</v>

112
00:11:25.163 --> 00:11:34.212
<v Daniel>And they found a massive subjective bias. People strongly prefer human advice and actively reject AI advice once the curtain is pulled back.</v>

113
00:11:34.487 --> 00:11:37.488
<v Maya>So we feel betrayed when we realize the empathy is hollow.</v>

114
00:11:37.548 --> 00:11:48.391
<v Daniel>Totally betrayed. So there is this profound irony here. The AI is incredibly good at providing the exact emotional support people crave, but humans reject it if they know the source.</v>

115
00:11:48.691 --> 00:11:55.973
